    "text": "IBM uses someone returning dumbbells for being too heavy to explain where AI agents are actually useful.\n\nYou see, a situation like a normal refund is easy to automate. Check the purchase date and the return policy, then approve or decline. You already know the conditions. Code can check them.\n\nThe dumbbell complaint needs a different kind of decision. You have to understand what the customer is saying and whether the reason makes sense. That's where the agent earns its place.\n\nI think about outbound the same way.\n\nA company hiring is a fact. Whether that hire suggests a problem we can help with takes judgment.\n\nYou need to read the role, understand the company and connect it to what you sell. A keyword match won't do all of that.\n\nThat's how we split the work in the OpenAI dots build below.\n\nhttps://t.co/shO8OijHTg brings in the buying signals. The dot researches the company, decides whether there's a reason to reach out, and writes two versions with the evidence attached.\n\nYou can read the source beside the message and see where its reasoning came from.\n\nThe code checks approval, the daily cap, the never-contact lists and whether the system is paused before anything can launch through Overloop AI.\n\nYou can also change how the dot writes without rebuilding the workflow.\n\nOur playbook lets it mention a public hiring post, for example. A connection with a competitor's salesperson is used for timing only. The message has to open on something relevant to the person's work.\n\nTo try it, leave Dry-Run on. Read the reasons behind the first leads it skips. Review a draft and check the saved message in Overloop before switching anything live.\n\nThat gives you something specific to fix. A weak lead needs better judgment. An awkward opener needs a better writing rule. Neither should require loosening the permission to send.\n\nThe article below walks through the full setup.",
