    "text": "You can turn Claude into a prospecting analyst with five copy-paste prompts, a browser and a spreadsheet.\n\n> Start with your offer and one buying signal.\n> You get a shortlist with sources and a reason behind every account. \n> The ones ready for outreach also get a first email.\n\nIn the article I walk through a real job post. It's a company hiring its first outbound rep to work on account selection and the prospecting playbook.\n\nIf you sell outbound consulting, that looks like the perfect reason to reach out. It may also be the best reason they won't need you. They're hiring someone to build it in-house.\n\nSo my call was HOLD, until I'd checked who owns that decision and whether the job post is still current.\n\nThe prompts ask for that kind of objection up front. Run them in order, each one feeds the next:\n\n1. The signal brief\nGive it your offer, who it's for, who usually buys and who's a bad fit.\nIt comes back with:\n- three changes you can verify\n- the decision the company may now face\n- the strongest reason your offer might not be needed\nIt isn't allowed to guess budget or urgency from an event.\n\n2. The search plan\nIt turns the brief into search phrases and the original pages to open.\nFor every candidate, save the URL, the exact excerpt, when it happened and when you checked.\nA search snippet never counts as proof.\n\n3. The account brief\nOne account at a time. It gives you:\n- facts, each tied to a source\n- the decision they might be facing\n- the evidence against it\n- the buyer, kept as a guess until you verify them\nThen ADVANCE, HOLD or PASS. No message for a HOLD or a PASS.\n\n4. The first email\nOnly for ADVANCE accounts.\nUp to 70 words: one observation you can back up, a resource you can actually send, one simple question.\nThen it maps every factual claim back to its source.\n\n5. The weekly review\nLog why you passed on each account: wrong company type, stale source, wrong person.\nIf your search keeps returning agencies and you sell to software companies, fix the search.\nClaude hands you a revised signal brief for the next round.\n\nFor your first run, pick one offer, one signal and ten accounts.\n\nOnce it's a weekly habit, max can take over the collecting.\nIt's the signal side of Overloop AI, which we're building.\nFor job posts, max watches careers pages and job boards.\n\nI put all five prompts in the article below, ready to copy.\n\n(bookmark for later)",
