    "text": "Most bad AI outbound comes from one thing: the model can't see what you know.\n\nI turned Claude into a prospecting analyst with five prompts (in the article below). Here's what we learned to tell it, so the emails come out right.\n\nIf it makes things up:\n\n1. Give it the actual page, the job post, the funding announcement. Don't let it work from a search snippet. That's where the congratulations email for a round nobody raised comes from.\n\n2. Ask it to put the source next to every fact. If it can't point to one, delete the fact.\n\n3. Keep two dates on every fact: when it happened, and when you found it. A page you found this morning can be about last spring.\n\nThe job post in the article had no date at all, so part of the hold was checking the role was still open.\n\n4. Tell it what it's not allowed to guess. Ours can't guess budget, urgency or whether someone is ready to buy from a single event. A funding round that says nothing about sales is a weak reason to pitch sales.\n\nIf every email could go to anyone\n\n5. Give it the reason you'd matter to them this week. Twenty cold emails a day can name the city I posted from. Not one of them knows if I have a problem.\n\n6. Be exact. Which role they reposted, how much they raised, what the tender actually says. \"They fit our ICP\" gives it nothing to write with.\n\n7. A test I like: cut the line about what you found and read the email again. If it still sounds like a decent email, send it. If it falls apart, that one line was doing all the work.\n\n8. Keep a short list of things it may never write, next to the instructions. A model can follow every tone instruction and still write the exact email buyers delete on sight.\n\nOurs started as four lines: no buzzwords, no em dashes, no fake urgency or \"circling back\", no \"Hi {{firstName}}\". It's longer now, and it does more than the instructions do.\n\nIf it pitches the wrong person\n\n9. The person in the news isn't always the person who buys. A company posts its first SDR role. The person they hire is nobody to you.\n\nWhoever runs the sales team is probably who you want, and even that stays a guess until someone has checked. Never let it make up a name.\n\n10. Ask it to make the case against the lead in the same answer. A company hiring its first outbound rep looks like the perfect lead for outbound consulting.\n\nIt's also a company that just decided to build it in-house. My call on that one was hold.\n\nIf it opens with what it found\n\n11. \"Saw you raised your Series B\" tells them a tool found it. Counting their pricing-page visits out loud is worse. What you found sets the timing and the topic. The first line is about something they did in public.\n\nIf it says yes to everyone\n\n12. Let it say \"not yet\". Ours says hold when the fit looks right but something still needs checking, and nothing goes out until a person has checked it. A reason to stop is a useful answer.\n\n13. Keep the first email small. 70 words at most: one thing you can back up, one thing you can actually send, one simple question. If what you're sending is a blank template, call it a template.\n\nIf it never gets better\n\n14. Log the reason behind every no, in one line. \"Wrong quarter\" is a date to come back. \"Already with a competitor\" is a different angle later. \"Not the buyer\" is a contact to fix. Same word, three different next moves.\n\n15. When the answer is wrong, change what it can see before you rewrite the instruction. Anthropic's own prompting video gets Claude from a wrong insurance ruling to the right one without really touching the instruction. Most bad answers are missing a fact.\n\nAll five prompts are in the article below. Copy them as they are.\n\n(bookmark for later)",
